```

Careful with rootMargin causing no entries intersecting at times. A robust approach: use rootMargin '-45% 0px -50% 0px' so a thin band in the middle is the "active" zone; threshold 0 means any intersection with that band triggers isIntersecting. Then set active to the intersecting id. But multiple could intersect. Simpler and robust: keep a map of visibility ratios and pick the max.

Let me implement:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const elements = navItems
    .map((item) => document.getElementById(item.id))
    .filter((el): el is HTMLElement => el !== null)

  if (elements.length === 0) return

  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      for (const entry of entries) {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          setActiveId(entry.target.id)
        }
      }
    },
    { rootMargin: '-45% 0px -50% 0px', threshold: 0 },
  )

  elements.forEach((el) => observer.observe(el))
  return () => observer.disconnect()
}, [])
```

With rootMargin -45% top and -50% bottom, the band is 5% of viewport height in the middle. Only one section can be in it at a time generally (sections are tall). This works nicely. But the hero section is min-height 100vh so it always covers the band when at top... at page top, hero spans whole viewport, so band is inside hero → active 'intro'. Good.

Edge: if the band falls between sections (gap), no entry intersects → active stays at last. Fine.

Now the hero is min-height 100vh so the band always inside it when scrolled near top. Good.

Now, TS: `entry.target.id` — target is Element, has id. OK.

Also `filter((el): el is HTMLElement => el !== null)` — getElementById returns HTMLElement | null, so el is already HTMLElement. Simplify: `.filter((el): el is HTMLElement => el !== null)` works fine.

